$r \propto n^{2}$
ie,

\frac{r_{f}}{r_{i}}=\left(\frac{n_{f}}{n_{i}}\right)^{2}

\begin{array}{l}
\Rightarrow \frac{21.2 \times 10^{-11}}{5.3 \times 10^{-11}}=\left[\frac{n}{1}\right]^{2} \\
\Rightarrow \quad n^{2}=4 \\
\Rightarrow \quad n=2
\end{array}

Given, ground state energy of hydrogen atom

E_{1}=-13.6 \mathrm{eV}

Energy of electron in first excited state

\begin{array}{l}
\text { (ie, } n=2 \text { ) } \\
\qquad E_{2}=-\frac{13.6}{(2)^{2}} \mathrm{eV}
\end{array}

Therefore, excitation energy

\begin{aligned}
\Delta E &=E_{2}-E_{1} \\
&=-\frac{13.6}{4}-(-13.6) \\
&=-3.4+13.6=10.2 \mathrm{eV}
\end{aligned}
